\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A stable prism line and a partial eigenspace}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{In the [twisted prism](fdip-0052), let #{e_u} and #{e_l} be
the normalized constant vectors on the upper and lower triangles.
The group #{\Gamma=C_3} fixes each, and its trivial isotypic block
has multiplicity two. In this orthonormal basis the precision and
covariance are
##{\begin{aligned}
Q_{\mathrm{triv}}
 &=\begin{pmatrix}5/2&-3/2\\-3/2&5/2\end{pmatrix},\\
Q_{\mathrm{triv}}^{-1}
 &=\begin{pmatrix}5/8&3/8\\3/8&5/8\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Their product is the identity; the determinant of the first is
#{4}. The other isotypic block is orthogonal and uncoupled.}

\p{Select #{X=\mathbb R e_u}, the line of fields constant on the
upper triangle and zero on the lower one. This line is
#{\Gamma}-stable, but
#{Qe_u=(5/2)e_u-(3/2)e_l\notin X}.
Its compressed covariance is #{5/8}, so
##{S_P=\frac85,\qquad PQ|_X=\frac52.}
Equivalently, eliminating the lower constant line gives
##{\frac52-\frac{(3/2)^2}{5/2}=\frac85.}
Thus this subrepresentation induces a different precision. By
contrast, the lines spanned by #{e_u+e_l} and #{e_u-e_l} have
precision eigenvalues #{1} and #{4}, respectively. Their
orthogonal selectors restrict, with retained precisions #{1} and
#{4}. This directly verifies the
[multiplicity-subspace criterion](fdip-005N).}

\p{The word \em{eigenvectors} in that criterion cannot be replaced
by \em{whole eigenspaces}. Let the trivial group act on the unit
four-cycle. The [precision](fdip-005F) has eigenvalue #{3} on the
two-dimensional space spanned by
##{v=(1,0,-1,0),\qquad w=(0,1,0,-1).}
Direct multiplication gives #{Qv=3v} and #{Qw=3w}. The selector
onto #{\mathbb R v} is proper within this eigenspace and restricts,
with #{S_P=3} in the unit coordinate #{v/\sqrt2}.
Every subspace is stable under the trivial group, but only
#{Q}-invariant ones meet the restriction criterion.}
